<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friday Mosque of Herat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Great Mosque of Herat, Masjid-i Jami of Herat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Friday Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> is the principal congregational mosque of Herat in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. An earlier mosque on the site was rebuilt by the Ghurid sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyath-al-din-muhammad">Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad</a> around 1200, and a Ghurid portal survives. It was repaired and redecorated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a> and later rulers, and its extensive tilework, much of it renewed in the twentieth century, makes it one of the city's landmarks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friday-mosque-of-herat.jpg" alt="Friday Mosque of Herat" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: koldo hormaza from madrid, españa · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friday_Mosque_in_Herat,_Afghanistan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The mosque stands on ground where religious buildings had been located for many centuries. The first known building was a Zoroastrian temple converted into a mosque in the 7th century; it was later enlarged by the Ghaznavids, and in the second half of the 11th century a smaller mosque with a wooden roof was founded under the Khwarazmian dynasty. An earthquake in 1102 almost destroyed it, and a later fire ruined the rebuilt mosque. The Ghurids seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in 1175, and at the end of the 12th century Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyath-al-din-muhammad">Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad</a> chose the plot of the burned mosque and additional land in the elevated north-eastern quarter, which had a direct water supply for its fountain.</p><p>When he died in 1203 he was buried in a mausoleum in the mosque. His son Sultan Ghayath-ul-din Mahmood continued the work, which was completed in 1210 with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> added.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Ghurids built the original mosque entirely of brick on a four-iwan plan around a courtyard with a water basin, now disappeared. The qibla faces west, about 20 degrees off the true direction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. The present complex is 180 metres long and 120 metres wide, with 460 domes, 444 pillars and 12 minarets, grouped around a courtyard 82 by 60 metres. At the south-east corner stands the Ghurid portal, which has not served as an entrance since unknown times; its brick mosaic and terracotta Kufic inscriptions, glazed blue on the front, survive. Stucco ornament of the Ghurid period has been uncovered in the southern and western iwans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in 1221 the mosque fell into ruin, and rebuilding began only after 1245 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shams-al-din-muhammad-kart">Shams al-Din Kart</a>. An earthquake in 1364 nearly destroyed it again. The last Kart ruler commissioned a bronze basin in 1375 that is still in the mosque.</p><p>Under the Timurid Shah Rukh (1405 to 1444) the courtyard facades received glazed tile mosaic and a marble mihrab, and Mir Ali-Shir Nava'i, adviser to Sultan Husayn Bayqara, remodelled it with a marble minbar and more tilework. The Timurid period corresponds to the time of its highest magnificence.</p><p>A major renovation took place in 1942 and 1943, with a tile studio established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>, and a fuller reconstruction followed from 1951 to 1973 that left little of the original Ghurid plasterwork or Timurid decoration visible. In the Soviet-Afghan War a rocket brought down a minaret in 1986, killing many people, and repairs were not finished until 1995. During the Taliban's rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> from 1996 to 2001 the mosque was closed to non-Muslims. The roofs were renovated in 2002, and parts of old Ghurid decoration were found in 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Mosque of Herat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ghiyath al-Din rebuilt the <b>Friday Mosque of Herat</b>, and Aybak began the Qutb Minar at Delhi, which Iltutmish completed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
